From fa665a7b5a14773184cb27466599398698f6358a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 21:34:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] nvidia-drm: HDMI 3D frame packing through NVKMS

NVKMS has HDMI 3D, frame packing only, and its display hardware does the
packing: NVKMS reads the HDMI 3D block of the EDID, validates a mode with
hdmi3D set (doubled pixel clock), programs the head for frame packing, takes
one surface per eye and sends its own HDMI vendor specific infoframe.
nvidia-drm reaches none of it: it does not set stereo_allowed, its mode list
never has a mode with a 3D layout, it ignores the 3D layout of a mode, and a
flip only hands over the left eye. A DRM client has no HDMI 3D on NVIDIA.

Make nvidia-drm behave like the DRM drivers that have it (i915), with that
path of NVKMS and nothing else:

 * Mode list. NVKMS already validates each EDID mode that the display
   advertises as frame packed a second time with hdmi3D enabled, and says
   so (hdmi3DAvailable in the reply of NVKMS_IOCTL_VALIDATE_MODE_INDEX).
   KAPI now passes that on, and nv_drm_connector_get_modes() adds, right
   after such a mode, a copy with DRM_MODE_FLAG_3D_FRAME_PACKING. HDMI
   connectors set stereo_allowed, so that the probe helper keeps the copies.
   For a display without HDMI 3D no mode is added: the list is what it was.

 * Modeset. A mode with the frame packing layout sets a new hdmi3D flag in
   the KAPI mode. KAPI then doubles the pixel clock of the NvKmsMode
   (nvKmsUpdateNvModeTimingsForHdmi3D()) and asks for NVKMS_STEREO_HDMI_3D
   in the mode validation parameters of that head, and of that head only:
   under that stereo mode NVKMS rejects the modes that are not frame packed.
   NVKMS does the rest. It checks the mode against the 3D block of the EDID
   again, programs the frame packed head, keeps the 2D VIC in the AVI
   infoframe and sends the frame packing vendor specific infoframe. With
   the next 2D mode it stops sending that infoframe.

 * Flip. As in the other drivers, the client provides one framebuffer of
   vdisplay + vtotal lines: left eye, active space, right eye. The hardware
   takes a memory offset per eye, and the size, the pitch, the format and
   the memory layout once for both. So each eye becomes an NVKMS surface of
   vdisplay lines over the memory of the framebuffer, the right eye one
   pitch * vtotal bytes further. They are created the first time the
   framebuffer is used in such a mode and destroyed with it. The primary
   layer is configured for one eye, and the two surfaces are flipped as a
   pair.

That mapping is exact only in some cases. Everything else fails the atomic
check, so that the client can fall back, and nothing is left half done:

 - the framebuffer has to be created with DRM_FORMAT_MOD_LINEAR and a
   single plane RGB format. In a block linear surface the lines are stored
   in blocks of 8 lines or more, and the right eye (line 1125 of a 1080p
   picture, 750 of a 720p one) does not start on a block;
 - NVKMS only registers a surface at an offset that is a multiple of 1024
   bytes. At 4 bytes per pixel, with a pitch of 5120 bytes (1280 pixels)
   every line is; with 7680 (1920 pixels) line 1125 is not. A 1080p frame
   packed framebuffer needs a pitch that is a multiple of 1024 bytes, that
   is, a buffer 2048 pixels wide of which 1920 are shown;
 - the primary plane only, covering exactly hdisplay x (vdisplay + vtotal),
   unscaled and unrotated; no cursor, no overlay, no asynchronous flip;
 - an HDMI connector whose display advertised frame packing when its modes
   were last probed;
 - no NV_HDMI_VSIF_METADATA. That property replaces the vendor specific
   infoframe of NVKMS, which is what tells the display that the picture is
   frame packed: the two cannot be active together, and the commit that
   would make them so is refused.

With a mode that has no frame packing layout the changed functions do what
they did. What was added to them is a test, of that layout or of a field
that is only set for such a mode, in front of code that lives in functions
of its own. What always runs is this: the two new flags of a KAPI mode are
cleared, and NVKMS acts on hdmi3D only when it is set; the connector
records whether its display advertised frame packing; and HDMI connectors
set stereo_allowed, which the probe helper looks at only for a mode with a
3D layout.

What a client can see that it could not: where the display advertises
frame packing, a client that asked for 3D modes (DRM_CLIENT_CAP_STEREO_3D)
finds the frame packed copies in the list of modes. A client that did not
ask is given the list it was given before.

The two surfaces of a framebuffer are made in the atomic check, so also by
a commit that only tests (DRM_MODE_ATOMIC_TEST_ONLY), and stay with the
framebuffer until it is destroyed. A framebuffer that was used in one
frame packed mode can be used again only in a mode with the same lines
(vdisplay and vtotal).

A mode with another 3D layout (side by side, top and bottom) is not listed
and, as before, is driven as its 2D mode.

Compiled with the compiler and the flags of the build: every object of
nvidia-drm.ko, and the sources of nvidia-modeset.ko that depend on the
changed header; both modules linked. Not run on a GPU; never seen on a 3D
display.
